{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat task.md history.md current_code.py'","aggregated_output":"# Google QUEST Q&A Labeling\n\nLink: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/google-quest-challenge  (overview, data and evaluation tabs)\nMetric direction: higher is better\n\n## Official description (overview + evaluation + data)\n(source: MLE-bench `description.md`, github.com/openai/mle-bench)\n\n# Overview\n## Description\nComputers are really good at answering questions with single, verifiable answers. But, humans are often still better at answering questions about opinions, recommendations, or personal experiences.\n\nHumans are better at addressing subjective questions that require a deeper, multidimensional understanding of context - something computers aren't trained to do well…yet.. Questions can take many forms - some have multi-sentence elaborations, others may be simple curiosity or a fully developed problem. They can have multiple intents, or seek advice and opinions. Some may be helpful and others interesting. Some are simple right or wrong.\n\n![Human computable dimensions](https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/google-research/human_computable_dimensions_1.png)\n\nUnfortunately, it’s hard to build better subjective question-answering algorithms because of a lack of data and predictive models. That’s why the [CrowdSource](https://crowdsource.google.com/csf/#/) team at Google Research, a group dedicated to advancing NLP and other types of ML science via crowdsourcing, has collected data on a number of these quality scoring aspects.\n\nIn this competition, you’re challenged to use this new dataset to build predictive algorithms for different subjective aspects of question-answering. The question-answer pairs were gathered from nearly 70 different websites, in a \"common-sense\" fashion. Our raters received minimal guidance and training, and relied largely on their subjective interpretation of the prompts. As such, each prompt was crafted in the most intuitive fashion so that raters could simply use their common-sense to complete the task. By lessening our dependency on complicated and opaque rating guidelines, we hope to increase the re-use value of this data set. What you see is what you get!\n\nDemonstrating these subjective labels can be predicted reliably can shine a new light on this research area. Results from this competition will inform the way future intelligent Q&A systems will get built, hopefully contributing to them becoming more human-like.\n\n## Evaluation\n\nSubmissions are evaluated on the mean column-wise [Spearman's correlation coefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spearman%27s_rank_correlation_coefficient). The Spearman's rank correlation is computed for each target column, and the mean of these values is calculated for the submission score.\n\nFor each qa_id in the test set, you must predict a probability for each target variable. The predictions should be in the range [0,1]. The file should contain a header and have the following format:\n\n```\nqa_id,question_asker_intent_understanding,...,answer_well_written\n6,0.0,...,0.5\n8,0.5,...,0.1\n18,1.0,...,0.0\netc.\n```\n\n## Timeline\n\n- February 3, 2020 - Team Merger deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.\n- February 3, 2020 - Entry deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.\n- February 10, 2020 - Final submission deadline.\n\nAll deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.\n\n## Prizes\n\n- 1st place - $10,000\n- 2nd place - $7,000\n- 3rd place - $5,000\n- 4th place - $2,000\n- 5th place - $1,000\n\n## Notebooks Requirements\n\nSubmissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. Your Notebook will re-run automatically against an unseen test set, and needs to output a file named submission.csv. You can still train a model offline, upload it as a dataset, and use the notebook exclusively to perform inference.\n\nIn order for the \"Submit to Competition\" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:\n\n  - CPU Notebooks <= 9 hours run-time\n  - GPU Notebooks <= 2 hours run-time\n  - Internet must be turned off\n\nIn synchronous Notebooks-only competitions, note that a submission that results in an error--either within the notebook or within the process of scoring--will count against your daily submission limit and will not return the specific error message. This is to limit probing the private test set.\n\nPlease note - committing your Notebook will result in a full execution of your code from top-to-bottom.\n\nPlease see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#kernels-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit.\n\n## Citation\n\nDanicky, Praveen Paritosh, Walter Reade, Addison Howard, Mark McDonald. (2019). Google QUEST Q&A Labeling. Kaggle. https://kaggle.com/competitions/google-quest-challenge\n\n# Dataset Description\n\nThe data for this competition includes questions and answers from various StackExchange properties. Your task is to predict target values of 30 labels for each question-answer pair.\n\nThe list of 30 target labels are the same as the column names in the `sample_submission.csv` file. Target labels with the prefix `question_` relate to the `question_title` and/or `question_body` features in the data. Target labels with the prefix `answer_` relate to the `answer` feature.\n\nEach row contains a single question and a single answer to that question, along with additional features. The training data contains rows with some duplicated questions (but with different answers). The test data does not contain any duplicated questions.\n\nThis is not a binary prediction challenge. Target labels are aggregated from multiple raters, and can have continuous values in the range `[0,1]`. Therefore, predictions must also be in that range.\n\nSince this is a synchronous re-run competition, you only have access to the Public test set. For planning purposes, the re-run test set is no larger than 10,000 rows, and less than 8 Mb uncompressed.\n\nAdditional information about the labels and collection method will be provided by the competition sponsor in the forum.\n\n## File descriptions\n\n  - **train.csv** - the training data (target labels are the last 30 columns)\n  - **test.csv** - the test set (you must predict 30 labels for each test set row)\n  - **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format; column names are the 30 target labels\n\n\n## Summary from our notes\nGoogle QUEST Q&A Labeling (Kaggle, 2019-20). For 6,079 StackExchange question-answer pairs (question_title, question_body, answer, plus category/host), predict 30 continuous quality labels (e.g. question_asker_intent _understanding, answer_helpful) that are averages of crowd ratings and take a few discrete values. Metric: mean Spearman correlation over the 30 columns, higher is better. Solutions fine-tune BERT-family models on question and answer text, and post-process predictions (rounding to the discrete levels) because Spearman depends on ranks.\n# Current version and earlier versions\n\nCurrent version: **v17**, leaderboard score 0.37427 (higher is better).\n\n## Earlier versions (oldest first; goal of the change, summary of the change, score the version reached)\nv1 -> v2: Fix file copy commands so required input files are actually copied into the working directory (mv -> cp -r) to allow the notebook to run on Kaggle. | change: Replaced three shell 'mv' commands with 'cp -r' for copying contents from /kaggle/input paths into the notebook working directory. | score after: 0.36973\nv2 -> v3: Switch to the shared evaluator implementation and point to a different experiment checkpoint / input library to improve inference results (rank blending). | change: Replaced in-file CrossEncoderEvaluator class with an import from kaggle_google_qa_labeling.evaluator; changed which input helper libraries are copied into the working dir; updated experiment_dir to a different checkpoint name; added a directory listi | score after: 0.37770 (+0.00797)\nv3 -> v4: Turn off rank blending in the evaluator to test single-model prediction behavior and see if disabling ensemble rank averaging improves the submission score. | change: Toggled CrossEncoderEvaluator parameter blend_ranks from True to False (disabled rank blending / rank averaging in the prediction pipeline). | score after: 0.37851 (+0.00081)\nv4 -> v5: Point the inference evaluator to a different experiment directory (i.e. a different saved checkpoint) to use that model for predictions—likely to try to improve leaderboard score. | change: Updated experiment_dir Path from '2019_12_13__06_50_00__563' to '2019_12_13__16_19_02__382' so the CrossEncoderEvaluator loads a different experiment/checkpoint. | score after: n/a\nv5 -> v6: Fix the input path to copy the saved experiment artifacts (pretrained model/checkpoint) into the working directory so inference can run. | change: Changed the copy source from /kaggle/input/google-qa-experiments/* to /kaggle/input/googleqaexperiments/* (correcting the input path where experiments are stored) and removed a no-op ls command. | score after: 0.38156\nv6 -> v7: Use a different saved experiment/checkpoint (and correct input path) so the evaluator runs with the new pretrained model; also list the experiments directory for verification. | change: Fixed the input copy path (added hyphens), added an ls to show the experiments directory, and switched experiment_dir to a different saved experiment ID (changed '2019_12_13__16_19_02__382' → '2019_12_13__21_24_38__891'). | score after: n/a\nv7 -> v8: Re-run the unchanged inference notebook to produce a submission CSV (no code changes). | change: No code changes (empty diff). The notebook was identical to the previous version — likely re-executed to generate predictions and a Kaggle submission. | score after: 0.38615\nv8 -> v9: Switch to a different experiment checkpoint for inference and correct which library subfolders are copied into the working directory so the evaluator and helper code are available. | change: Refined cp commands to copy specific subpackages (kaggle_google_qa_labeling, prompter, experiments/*) instead of copying whole input directories; removed an ls line; changed experiment_dir timestamp string to point to a different saved experiment (lo | score after: n/a\nv9 -> v10: Switch the dataset utilities/class to the cross-dataset variant and change the inference blending method to a sigmoid strategy to improve/standardize prediction blending. | change: Replaced imports from JoinedDataset/utilities to CrossDataset/cross_dataset_utilities and changed CrossEncoderEvaluator instantiation from blend_ranks=False to blend_strategy='sigmoid'. | score after: n/a\nv10 -> v11: No code changes; re-run or re-save the same inference notebook to produce a submission / record the score. | change: Identical code — no changes in files (NOOP). Likely a rerun or checkpoint save that produced the new public score. | score after: 0.38408\nv11 -> v12: Exclude a subset of saved models by deleting their folders so they are not loaded in the inference/ensemble (cleanup and ensemble composition tweak). | change: Added shell rm -rf commands to delete model_5 through model_9 directories under the experiment's models folder. | score after: 0.37988 (-0.00419)\nv12 -> v13: Use rank-based blending instead of sigmoid blending to improve the final ensemble inference; also avoid deleting model directories during setup. | change: Replaced CrossEncoderEvaluator(..., blend_strategy='sigmoid') with blend_strategy='rank' to switch blending method; removed multiple rm -rf lines that deleted model folders. | score after: 0.38367 (+0.00379)\nv13 -> v14: Point the notebook to a different experiments input path so inference uses a different set of saved experiments / pretrained weights. | change: Changed the copy source path for experiments from /kaggle/input/google-qa-experiments/kaggle_google_qa_labeling/experiments/* to /kaggle/input/googleqaexperiments/experiments/*, switching which saved experiment files (pretrained checkpoints) are used | score after: 0.38367 (+0.00000)\nv14 -> v15: Switch to a BiEncoder-based evaluator and run predictions using multiple fold models to improve the blended submission score. | change: Updated input/copy paths, replaced CrossEncoderEvaluator with BiEncoderEvaluator, changed experiment directory literal, and configured the evaluator to use 'fold_models' (enabling fold averaging). | score after: 0.38484 (+0.00117)\nv15 -> v16: Change the ensemble blending method from rank-based averaging to sigmoid blending to try to improve final predictions (and add/remove commented cleanup commands). | change: Updated BiEncoderEvaluator initialization to use blend_strategy='sigmoid' instead of 'rank'. Also added several commented-out shell rm -rf lines targeting fold model directories (cleanup notes). | score after: 0.38513 (+0.00028)\nv16 -> v17: Enable removal of existing fold_models directories at the start of the notebook to ensure a clean workspace / free disk space before inference. | change: Uncommented six shell commands that run `!rm -rf` on fold_models/model_0 through model_5 so the directories are actually deleted at runtime (previously commented out). | score after: 0.37427 (-0.01086)\n# %%\n!cp -r /kaggle/input/google-qa-libs/projects/kaggle_google_qa_labeling/kaggle_google_qa_labeling ./\n!cp -r /kaggle/input/google-qa-libs/projects/prompter/prompter ./\n!cp -r /kaggle/input/google-qa-experiments/projects/kaggle_google_qa_labeling/experiments/* ./\n!cp -r /kaggle/input/transformersdependencies/dependencies/* ./\n\n# %%\n!rm -rf ./2019_12_15__12_28_32__384/fold_models/model_0/\n!rm -rf ./2019_12_15__12_28_32__384/fold_models/model_1/\n!rm -rf ./2019_12_15__12_28_32__384/fold_models/model_2/\n!rm -rf ./2019_12_15__12_28_32__384/fold_models/model_3/\n!rm -rf ./2019_12_15__12_28_32__384/fold_models/model_4/\n!rm -rf ./2019_12_15__12_28_32__384/fold_models/model_5/\n\n# %%\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nfrom torch.utils.data.dataloader import DataLoader\n\nfrom prompter.Factory import Factory\nfrom prompter.learner.Learner import Learner\n\nfrom kaggle_google_qa_labeling.evaluator.Evaluator import Evaluator\nfrom kaggle_google_qa_labeling.dataset.cross_dataset_utilities import *\nfrom kaggle_google_qa_labeling.dataset.BiDataset import BiDataset\nfrom kaggle_google_qa_labeling.evaluator.BiEncoderEvaluator import BiEncoderEvaluator\n\n# %%\ntest_df = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/google-quest-challenge/test.csv')\nsubm_df = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/google-quest-challenge/sample_submission.csv')\n\n# %%\nexperiment_dir = Path('2019_12_15__12_28_32__384')\nevaluator = BiEncoderEvaluator(experiment_dir=experiment_dir, device='cuda', bs=16, blend_strategy='sigmoid', models_dir_name='fold_models')\n\n# %%\ny_pred = evaluator.run(test_df)\n\n# %%\nout_df = pd.DataFrame(columns=subm_df.columns)\nout_df['qa_id'] = test_df['qa_id']\nout_df.iloc[:,1:] = y_pred\nout_df.to_csv('submission.csv', index=False)\n\n# %%\n\n\n# %%\n\n\n# %%\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
